Another reproducer of the same root cause, where the cast type is not used as the constructor's contextually expected type. This one has an `& readonly` target and covers list constructors as well as mapping constructors. Here the cast compiles but **panics at runtime** with `bad type cast` inside an ordinary function body:

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;
type R record {| int x; |};
public function main() {
    int[] a = <int[] & readonly>[1, 2];
    io:println(a); // @output [1,2]
    R r = <R & readonly>{x: 1};
    io:println(r); // @output {"x":1}
}
```

Actual (`main` @ 47f4de21aa):
```
error: bad type cast
        at main(f1-readonly-cast-ctx-v.bal:4)
```

Expected: `[1,2]` then `{"x":1}`.

`int[] & readonly c = <int[] & readonly>[1, 2];` panics the same way, even though the outer and cast types are identical. Casting a variable that already holds a read-only value (`<int[] & readonly>roVar`) works, which shows the constructor inside the cast is not built with the read-only expected type.

Spec §6.20: "The contextually expected type for expression is the intersection of the contextually expected type of the type-cast-expr and the type described by the type-descriptor." It also notes that "a type-cast-expr of `<readonly>` can be used both to cause constructors within the expression to construct values with the read-only bit on and to verify…". For mapping constructors: "If the applicable contextually expected type is a subtype of readonly, then the mapping will be constructed with its read-only bit on."

### Description

When an exclusive record type `record {| *OpenBase; ... |}` includes an inclusive (open) record type `record { ... }`, the implicit `anydata...` rest descriptor of the included type is lost, so the including type is treated as closed. An explicit rest descriptor in the included type is inherited correctly (corpus `subset5/05-record/inclusion-rest-v.bal`).

### Steps to Reproduce

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

type OpenBase record {
    int x;
};

type Derived record {|
    *OpenBase;
    string y;
|};

public function main() {
    Derived d = {x: 1, y: "hello", "z": 3};
    io:println(d); // @output {"x":1,"y":"hello","z":3}
}
```

Run with `go run ./cli/cmd run <file>.bal`

**Expected:**
```
{"x":1,"y":"hello","z":3}
```

**Actual:**
```
error[SEMANTIC_ERROR]: incompatible type: expected never, got 3
  --> inclusion-open-rest-v.bal:13:41
   |
13 |     Derived d = {x: 1, y: "hello", "z": 3};
   |                                         ^

ballerina: compilation contains errors
```

The runtime makes the same mistake: building `Derived` without `"z"`, viewing it as `map<anydata>` and setting `m["z"] = 3` panics with `inherent type violation`. Other value kinds also fail at compile time: `"extra": [1, 2]` gives `list type not found in expected type`.

**Spec reasoning:**

§5.4.2.2 record-type-inclusion: "The field-descriptors and any record-rest-descriptor are included the type being defined; the meaning is the same as if they had been specified explicitly." and "For the purposes of resolving a record-type-inclusion, an including or included type that is an inclusive-record-type-descriptor is treated as if it were the equivalent exclusive-record-type-descriptor with an explicit record-rest-descriptor." (`record { F }` ≡ `record {| F; anydata...; |}`). Also: "A record-rest-descriptor in the including type overrides any record-rest-descriptor in the included type". `Derived` has no rest descriptor of its own, so it is `record {| int x; string y; anydata...; |}`, and `"z": 3` is a valid extra field.

### Affected Version(s)

nutcracker (ballerina-nutcracker/ballerina) `main` @ 47f4de21aa0d12a7f050599bb97d19daf1692f7a

### OS, DB, other environment details and versions

Linux, Go 1.27.1

### Related area

Type checker
